10.text:
The experiment uses visible-light photosensitization (via the thioxanthenone) to drive covalent protein labeling that is subsequently detected by CuAAC with Cy5-azide. The first probe bears a phenol that readily forms a reactive phenoxyl radical under these conditions, producing a large light-dependent increase in gel fluorescence. Replacing the phenol with a benzyl alcohol removes the site of phenoxyl formation, sharply reducing the light-dependent signal. The residual, still-observable difference must therefore arise from direct action of the thioxanthenone itself (energy or electron transfer to proximal nucleophiles or weak C–H bonds), which is independent of the phenolic substituent.

Thus the molecule responsible for the fluorescent difference observed with the second probe is 2-fluoro-7-methoxy-9H-thioxanthen-9-one.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. The phenol-to-benzyl-alcohol swap eliminates phenoxyl-radical formation, so the residual light-dependent labeling must be driven by the thioxanthenone photosensitizer.}

